            "en": "In eighteenth-century France, speaking against the Catholic Church or criticizing royal corruption could lead to immediate imprisonment in the Bastille without trial. Amidst this climate of fear, François-Marie Arouet—known to history as Voltaire—wielded the sharpest pen in Europe. Exiled to England, he witnessed parliamentary debate, religious toleration, and Newtonian physics, contrasting them with French absolutist repression.",
